narrative:
## The Complete L2 Toolkit

You now have all the building blocks for data modeling:

| Concept | Syntax | When to Use |
|---------|--------|-------------|
| Struct | `type Name = { field: Type; }` | Grouping related values |
| Enum | `enum Name { Var, Var2 }` | One type, fixed choices |
| Enum with data | `Variant(data: Type)` | Choices that carry values |
| Method | `fn Type.method(self) { }` | Behavior on data |
| Nested types | Field of another struct/enum | Hierarchical data |
| Match | `match val { V => { } }` | Branch on variants |

### Domain 1: Bookstore

```xiom
type Book = {
  title: Str;
  price: Float64;
}

enum BookCondition {
  New,
  Used(wear: Str),
  Damaged,
}

fn BookCondition.discount(self) -> Float64 {
  match self {
    New => { return 0.0; }
    Used(_) => { return 0.3; }
    Damaged => { return 0.6; }
  }
}
```

### Domain 2: Fitness Tracker

```xiom
enum Exercise {
  Running(distance: Float64),
  Lifting(weight: Int, reps: Int),
  Yoga,
}

fn Exercise.calories(self) -> Int {
  match self {
    Running(dist) => { return (dist * 100.0) as Int; }
    Lifting(w, reps) => { return w * reps / 10; }
    Yoga => { return 150; }
  }
}
```

### Domain 3: Notification System

```xiom
enum Priority { Low, Medium, High, Urgent }

type Notification = {
  title: Str;
  body: Str;
  priority: Priority;
}

fn Notification.should_alert(self) -> Bool {
  return priority == Priority.High || priority == Priority.Urgent;
}
```

### The Pattern

Every domain follows the same recipe: identify the nouns (structs), the choices (enums), and the verbs (methods). Everything else is just combining these three concepts.

